															CRIME REPORTING SYSTEM (URSALA)

																	Project Overview

The Crime Reporting System is a secure, monolithic web application designed to bridge the communication gap between citizens and local law enforcement. Built with an emphasis on robust backend security and architectural separation of concerns.
The system allows citizens to seamlessly file and track incident reports while providing police officers with a protected, authenticated dashboard to investigate and update case statuses.
Software Engineering Architecture. This application strictly adheres to the Model-View-Controller (MVC) design pattern, further enhanced by a dedicated Service layer to encapsulate business logic.

->Presentation Layer (View): Vanilla HTML, CSS, and JavaScript served directly via Spring Boot's embedded Tomcat server, eliminating CORS overhead and simplifying deployment.
->Controller Layer: RESTful APIs utilizing ResponseEntity to enforce standard HTTP status codes (e.g., 201 Created, 404 Not Found).
->Service Layer: Handles core business logic and utilizes Java Optional to safely manage null references and data retrieval.
->Data Access Layer (Repository/Model): Spring Data JPA interfaces abstracting complex SQL queries, mapped to a dynamically generated H2 in-memory database. Entity timestamps are managed automatically via @PrePersist and @PreUpdate lifecycle callbacks.

>>Technology Stack
1> Backend: Java 25, Spring Boot (4.1.1)
2> Database: H2 In-Memory Database, Spring Data JPA / Hibernate
3> Security: Spring Security 6+
4> Frontend: HTML5, CSS3, ES6 JavaScript, Fetch API

Security Posture::
The application implements a defense-in-depth approach, balancing frontend obscurity with strict backend endpoint protection:
Stage 1: Frontend Gateway (Bot Deflection & Routing)
The root index.html acts as a zero-UI gateway requiring specific numeric keystrokes to access the hidden sub-systems. This mitigates basic automated bot submissions and unauthorized user routing.

Stage 2: Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)
Configured via Spring Security's Lambda DSL. Public endpoints (POST /api/reports, GET /api/reports/{id}) are exposed for citizen use. Administrative endpoints (GET /api/reports, PATCH /api/reports/**) are strictly locked behind a POLICE role requirement.

Stage 3: Cryptographic Hashing
In-memory authentication bypasses deprecated plaintext storage by utilizing BCryptPasswordEncoder to securely hash officer credentials upon application boot.

How to Run the Application::
1. Booting the Server
Ensure you have Java 25 installed. Run the application from your IDE or via Maven:
./mvnw spring-boot:run
The server will start on http://localhost:8080.

2. Navigating the Gateway
Open your browser and navigate to http://localhost:8080. You will be presented with a blank gateway screen. Type the following access codes directly on your keyboard to navigate the system:
   1800-100 -> Routes to the Citizen Submission Form.
   1800-1800 -> Routes to the Client Tracking Portal (requires a Report ID).
   32450 -> Routes to the Police Dashboard (Protected by HTTP Basic Auth).

3. Police Authentication
When viewing the Police Dashboard, the background JavaScript automatically injects the BCrypt-verified credentials to fetch the protected data.
	Username: officer
	Password: 32450